[00:36.45]Conversation One
[00:38.43]M: Hey, I'm a little low on cash these days.
[00:41.45]W: Really? What about getting a new job?
[00:44.30]M: No, there's still a good half-year before graduating.
[00:48.33]W: Yeah, you should spend it wisely. 
[00:50.69]This is the best chance to get a job.
[00:53.03]M: What do you mean?
[00:54.41]W: Well, I think this is your last chance to get a job before you graduate. 
[00:58.91]Or, what do you plan on after graduating? 
[01:01.92]Just hanging around? 
[01:03.64]Playing games?
[01:04.67]M: I don't know. 
[01:06.02]I have no idea what to do after graduation. 
[01:09.65]Do you have any plans?
[01:11.34]W: Well, for me, since the economy is going bad, like there is no opening at the moment, but I was thinking if I could keep doing my part-time job at the moment just until things start getting better. 
[01:25.13]Well, I guess you still have nine months left.
[01:27.98]M: Yeah. It's a little depressing though, thinking about graduation, being thrown out into the real world.
[01:35.38]W: All my friends who graduated, they're already regretting it. 
[01:39.47]They want to go back to school.
[01:41.43]M: I don't want to go to grad school.
[01:43.58]W: Why not?
[01:44.61]M: I don't know if I could deal with another four years of schooling.
[01:48.85]W: It's not four years. 
[01:50.60]It's only two years, and plus, like, I mean, if you're going to regret so much like getting a job, like, and plus the economy is really bad for a job right now, I think it's best to lay low and just, you know, maybe study a bit more till the economy gets better and then get a job.
[02:10.39]M: Well, if I could get some sort of full scholarship, perhaps I would be more inclined to do it, but...
[02:16.67]W: Yeah, yeah. It's not bad.
