[02:43.33]2. What does the woman plan to do?
[02:59.95]3. What does the man think of graduation?
[03:16.34]4. What does the woman advise the man to do?
[03:33.76]Conversation Two
[03:35.61]M: So, you've seen obviously life in France, family life in France, and now you've seen family life in America. 
[03:43.36]Is there any difference?
[03:45.15]W: Yes, there are some differences in education. 
[03:48.38]The education in France, parents are stricter than here.
[03:52.42]M: So you say that the parents are stricter in France. 
[03:56.07]Can you give me an example? 
[03:57.74]How are they stricter?
[03:59.17]W: Like, here it's rare when parents say ¡°no¡± to their kids, and they say, most of the time, ¡°yes¡± for almost everything.
[04:07.39]M: So, you're saying the kids are spoiled?
[04:09.64]W: A little bit, yeah, sometimes.
[04:11.87]M: What about the daily routine of family life? 
[04:14.68]Is there anything different in the daily routine?
[04:17.34]W: Here, in the US, we have a lot of babysitters, but in France, the parents, most of the time, one of the parents works, and the other takes care of the kids. 
[04:28.38]And here, in the US, I notice that both parents work all the time. 
[04:33.12]It's rare when the parents take care of the kids. 
[04:36.31]They contract a babysitter most of the time.
[04:39.30]M: So, now that you've been a babysitter for a while, would you recommend being a babysitter for other people?
[04:45.18]W: Yeah, sure, of course. 
[04:47.05]It's a very good experience, and it's very good for our resume, for people who want to finish their study, and to find work, and it's a good way to learn English.
[04:57.52]M: OK, well, it was nice talking to you.
[05:00.31]W: Thank you. You too.
[05:02.96]Questions 5 to 8 are based on the conversation you have just heard.
