[06:54.49]Passage One
[06:55.98]By now you may have heard Taylor Swift's heartbreaking song ¡°Ronan,¡± about the too-short life of a little boy. 
[07:03.17]Ms. Swift gave it an emotional performance on the Stand Up to Cancer television program on September 7, and it has been topping the charts ever since.
[07:13.79]The real boy behind the song is Ronan Thompson, a naughty 3-year-old boy from Phoenix with coppery blond hair and big blue eyes. 
[07:23.51]In 2010, a few months after his third birthday, Ronan was given a diagnosis of cancer, an often deadly childhood cancer. 
[07:32.73]He died on May 9, 2011, three days before he was to turn 4. 
[07:38.59]It's the most common cancer in infants and accounts for about 15% of all childhood cancer deaths.
[07:45.42]Ms. Swift learned about Ronan from a blog that his mother, Maya Thompson, began shortly after the diagnosis to keep family and friends informed. 
[07:55.26]Ms.Thompson called the blog Rockstar Ronan, but she did not tell directly about his illness, and her heartbreak was painfully evident in every update. 
[08:06.06]Ronan spent his last days in a hospital in Phoenix with his parents and brothers. 
[08:11.96]After he died, his mother continued to write tearful posts on her blog, which had turned into an open letter to her son.
[08:20.57]Cancer is the second leading cause of death of children under 15¡ªafter accidents¡ªyet childhood cancer receives only 3 to 4 percent of national cancer research funds. 
[08:33.23]Although childhood cancer is considered rare, an estimated 12,060 new cases are expected this year among children at the age of 14 and younger. 
[08:45.29]The survival rate for childhood cancer is about 80%.
[08:50.49]Questions 9 to 11 are based on the passage you have just heard.
[08:55.81]9. What is Taylor Swift's song ¡°Ronan¡± about?
[09:13.80]10. How did Taylor Swift learn about Ronan?
[09:30.35]11. What is the first leading cause of death of children under 15?
[09:49.86]Passage Two
[09:51.25]A study in Norway has found that students who stayed in school longer than others of their same age scored higher on intelligence tests.
[10:01.49]In the middle of the 1950s, the government began requiring students to attend school until the age of 16 instead of 14. 
[10:10.92]Communities had almost 20 years to make the change. 
[10:14.72]So some students went to school for 7 years while others went for at least 9 years. 
[10:20.42]This difference gave researchers the chance to see if the additional schooling had any effect on intellectual development. 
[10:29.16]The researchers compared the test results of 107,000 young men to their years of school. 
[10:35.77]Taryn Ann Galloway is a researcher at the University of Oslo. 
[10:40.17]She says that students who attended school for 9 years scored 7 points higher than those who attended for 7 years. 
[10:48.32]Those who went for 8 years scored about 4 points higher.
[10:52.43]Another recent study, in the journal Nature, found that IQ scores can rise or fall during the teenage years. 
[11:00.60]In 2004, researchers from University College London tested 33 young people aged from 12 to 16. 
[11:10.12]They repeated the test 4 years later. 
[11:12.89]They found increases or decreases of as much as 20 points. 
[11:17.39]Both times, they also took structural brain scans using MRI, magnetic resonance imaging. 
[11:24.55]The study found that as IQ scores increased, so did the density of gray matter in some areas of the students' brains. 
[11:33.12]Professor Cathy Price says the differences in performance could be the result of some teens being early or late developers. 
[11:41.11]But she says it is equally possible that education played a part.
[11:47.44]Questions 12 to 15 are based on the passage you have just heard.
[11:52.95]12. What did Norwegian government do in the middle of the 1950s?
[12:12.87]13. What did Galloway find in her research?
[12:30.89]14. Who were the subjects of the study in 2004?
[12:49.50]15. What was the change in the students' brains when their IQ scores increased?
[13:09.85]Section C 
